Exercise 4.8 is fixed-point induction: if \(S\) contains \(\bot\), contains \(f(x)\) whenever it contains \(x\), and contains the supremum of every increasing chain it contains, then \(\mathrm{fix}(f)\in S\); applied to \(S=\{x\mid a(x)=b(x)\}\) when \(a\) and \(b\) are approximable endomaps, \(a(\bot)=b(\bot)\), and \(f\circ a=a\circ f\) and \(f\circ b=b\circ f\). `supChain` is the directed union of a monotone chain indexed by the natural numbers, and `mem_supChain` says a neighbourhood lies in that union exactly when it lies in some term. `iterElem_zero` says the zero approximant is bottom, `iterElem_succ` says the successor approximant is the elementwise image of the previous one, and `iterElem_monotone` says the approximants increase. `fixElement_eq_supChain` identifies the least fixed point with the supremum of that chain by matching neighbourhood membership in both directions. `fix_induction` runs the three hypotheses along the approximants and then applies chain-closure to their supremum. `fix_induction_eq` takes the predicate to be agreement of \(a\) and \(b\): equality of the composites rewrites \(a(f(x))\) as \(f(a(x))\) and \(b(f(x))\) as \(f(b(x))\), the base case is the given agreement at bottom, and both images of a supremum are directed unions, so pointwise agreement moves membership witnesses either way. `example_408` is the conjunction of this equality and the induction principle. **Theorem:** yes — \(\mathrm{fix}(f)\) lies in every such \(S\), and in particular \(a\) and \(b\) agree at \(\mathrm{fix}(f)\). **Proof:** yes — induction on the approximants from bottom, using that the least fixed point is their supremum, checks the three closure conditions in the equality case.
